Chapter: DebugExprTest
  cites Works chapter DevConsole
  cites Works chapter DevConsoleBoot
  cites Works chapter DevConsoleMenu
  cites Works chapter UefiConsole
  cites Works chapter KeyManager
  cites Kernel chapter DiskFacts
  cites Foreword chapter FactStore
  cites Foreword chapter Sha256

 Address expressions reach the debugger through the key handler the
 console actually calls.

 Every line goes through `handle-console-input` in ModeDebug, and that is
 the point rather than a detail: `expr-eval` has its own test as a pure
 function, so what is left to prove is the WIRING -- that a line which is
 not a menu key reaches the evaluator, that a good one moves
 last-address, and that a bad one does not. Calling `expr-eval` directly
 here would pass against the arm that used to be `else state` and swallow
 the line, which is exactly the bug this closes.

 The last-address readings are the load-bearing ones. A status line can
 say anything; `$` is read back out of the state on the NEXT expression,
 so `$ + 16` proves the previous value was actually stored rather than
 printed and dropped.

 The refusal has to be checked the same way. After a bad expression the
 status changes -- so a test watching only the status would pass -- and
 what must NOT change is last-address. So the bad case is followed by a
 bare `$`, which reports what the cursor really is.

 The watch commands are here for the same reason and not to re-test
 `DevState`, which has its own test over memory it controls. What this
 half proves is that the console THREADS the returned table back into its
 own state: `watch.check` re-arms, and a dispatch that dropped the
 returned `DevState` would still print a plausible line while every later
 check reported against a stale snapshot. `armed-at-cursor` naming the
 address the expressions left behind is what ties the two features
 together -- the cursor an expression set is the region a watch arms on.

 We say:

Section: Fixtures

  bare-console : UefiConsoleState
  bare-console = UefiConsoleState {
    sys-table = 0,
    con-out = 0,
    con-in = 0,
    rows = 25,
    cols = 80,
    cursor-row = 0,
    cursor-col = 0
  }

  bare-disk : DiskFactStore
  bare-disk = DiskFactStore { superblock = empty-superblock, mem = fact-store-empty, base = 0, limit = 0 }

  bare-keys : Keypair
  bare-keys = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-debug-expr-test"))

Section: Reporting

  report : Text, DevConsoleState -> [Console] Nothing
  report (label) (s) = act
    print-line-uni (label & ": " & s.status)
  end

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    let s0 = dev-console-init bare-console bare-disk bare-keys
    in let d0 = dispatch-main-menu "debug" s0
    in act

      m <- handle-console-input d0 "mem"
      report "menu-key-still-works" m

      a <- handle-console-input d0 "heap-base + 0x100"
      report "named-plus-hex" a

      b <- handle-console-input a "$ + 16"
      report "dollar-reads-back" b

      bad <- handle-console-input b "nosuch + 1"
      report "refused" bad

      after <- handle-console-input bad "$"
      report "cursor-after-refusal" after

      w0 <- handle-console-input after "watch.check"
      report "no-watches-yet" w0

      w1 <- handle-console-input w0 "watch"
      report "armed-at-cursor" w1

      w2 <- handle-console-input w1 "watch.check"
      report "unchanged" w2

      w3 <- handle-console-input w2 "watch.clear"
      report "cleared" w3

      w4 <- handle-console-input w3 "watch.check"
      report "empty-again" w4
    end
  end
